;; TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; COMPILE-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv]: PASS
;; COMPILE-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv --differentiate]: PASS
;; SKIP-DEFAULT-PASS
;; HOIST-HARDWARE-PROFILE: bmg
;; VERIFY-AUTODIFF: A=4x16@0:0.01 at.A=0,2 expect.A=64.0 group=64 h=0.5 atol=5e-1 output-mat=4x16
;;
;; ENDEAVOUR 175 -- the VJP of reduce-workgroup.  TDD.
;;
;; THIS SPEC EXISTS TO DISCRIMINATE TWO CANDIDATE RULES BY A FACTOR OF 64.
;;
;; reduce-workgroup is an ALL-reduce: every thread ends up holding the total.  So the forward
;; map is not one output but N:
;;
;;     y_t = SUM over j of x_j      for every thread t
;;     dy_t/dx_j = 1                for all t, j
;;
;; and the VJP sums over OUTPUTS:
;;
;;     xbar_j = SUM over t of ybar_t
;;
;; That is an all-reduce of the adjoint -- an all-reduce is a fan-in followed by a fan-out, and
;; transposing reverses the order and dualises each half, giving fan-out then fan-in, which is
;; an all-reduce again.  It is SELF-TRANSPOSING, exactly as shuffle-xor is.
;;
;; THE TEMPTING WRONG RULE is xbar_j = ybar_j -- "the forward pass broadcast the result to every
;; thread, so the adjoint is already there".  That confuses the PRIMAL broadcast with the
;; adjoint, which flows the other way.  Here every one of the 64 threads writes the total to its
;; own output element, so sum(C) = 64 * total and the true derivative is 64.  The copy rule
;; would read 1.0.  A kernel where only ONE thread consumed the result would separate them the
;; other way round: the copy rule gives the correct value in that thread and ZERO in the other
;; 63.  Either way it is a wrong answer with no failure, which is why this spec pins a NUMBER
;; rather than merely compiling.
;;
;; atol is loose (5e-1) because a 64x fan-in amplifies the finite difference by the same factor.
;; The function is exactly linear in A, so the FD is still exact up to float rounding -- the
;; tolerance is absorbing scale, not sloppiness.
;;
;; MEASURED, and the measurement is why reduce-workgroup is an ANALYZED FORM rather than a
;; macro.  As a macro it expanded before the backward walk, and the ordinary machinery reversed
;; the expansion.  That is not merely heavy -- it is WRONG.  This spec read:
;;
;;     analytical=1.0   numerical=64.00012   diff=63.000122
;;
;; 1.0 is the derivative of the IDENTITY: the backward pass had lost the entire reduction.  The
;; AD walk models SINGLE-THREAD dataflow, and thread j writing sv[j] for thread i to read is a
;; CROSS-THREAD edge no per-thread reversal can see, so the fan-in simply vanished.  reduce-warp
;; differentiates correctly only because shuffle-xor carries an explicit VJP modelling the
;; cross-lane transpose; shared memory has no such rule.  See BUG 073.
;;
;; With the semantic VJP (an all-reduce of the adjoint, since an all-reduce is self-transposing)
;; the same spec reads analytical=64.0 against numerical=64.00012.
;;
;; The spec itself did not change between those two runs: it constrains the ANSWER, not the
;; implementation, which is what let it arbitrate the choice at all.

(def-type mat-t (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel diff_reduce_workgroup (A &out C)
  (declare #'(mat-t &out mat-t)
           (global-size :set-to 64)
           (local-size  :set-to 64))
  ;; EXPLICIT 4, not :match-num-warps-per-workgroup.  tests/verify-autodiff-runner.lisp
  ;; allocates the scratch buffer itself and, like the hoisters before BUG 070, accepts only a
  ;; NUMBER -- a symbolic size fails the run with ":MATCH-NUM-WARPS-PER-WORKGROUP is not of type
  ;; NUMBER".  It is a THIRD consumer of :size-expr that BUG 070 did not reach.  4 is correct
  ;; here (64 threads / 16-lane bmg warp) and keeps this spec about the DERIVATIVE; restore the
  ;; symbolic form when the runner grows a resolver.
  (let ((sv (make-scratch-vector float 4)))
    (let ((tid (to-int (get-local-linear-id))))
      (let ((row (/ tid 16))
            (col (rem tid 16)))
        (let ((v (~ A row col)))
          (reduce-workgroup #'+ v 0.0 :local-scratch-vec sv)
          ;; Every thread writes the total, so sum(C) = 64 * total.
          (set! (~ C row col) v))))))
